WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.140
The first film ended with phage DNA inside a bacterium, and the cell building phage

2
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.960
parts. So how does one phage, which never divides, become many?

3
00:00:15.083 --> 00:00:16.643
Scientists tested this.

4
00:00:17.823 --> 00:00:22.563
They infected bacteria with phages, then broke some cells open at different times.

5
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:25.703
Each time, they looked for whole phages.

6
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:31.247
Early in the infection, the cells already hold plenty of phage material.

7
00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:34.627
How many whole phages will they find inside?

8
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:45.303
None. There are phage parts, but no whole phages.

9
00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:48.503
So a phage is not split from a parent.

10
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:50.883
It is built from parts.

11
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:55.237
Later, the parts are put together into many new phages.

12
00:00:56.577 --> 00:00:57.817
Then the cell bursts open.

13
00:00:58.997 --> 00:01:01.537
The new phages escape, and the host cell dies.

14
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.570
This path is the lytic cycle.

15
00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.090
The phage takes over now.

16
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.970
The host builds new phages, and then the cell bursts to release them.

17
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:14.993
Some phages have a second path.

18
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:18.913
The best known is phage lambda, which infects E. coli.

19
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.213
Its DNA does not have to take over right away.

20
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:29.607
Lambda's orange DNA slips into the blue chromosome and becomes part of it.

21
00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:33.527
Stored viral DNA like this is called a prophage.

22
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.280
The cell stays alive and keeps dividing by binary fission.

23
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.800
Each time, it copies its chromosome, prophage and all.

24
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.380
Both new cells get a copy.

25
00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:48.723
Here is the counter from the first film.

26
00:01:50.103 --> 00:01:54.283
One cell, two, four, eight, sixteen, each one carrying the orange DNA.

27
00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:57.323
Now look at the second counter.

28
00:01:58.803 --> 00:02:05.323
New phages: zero. The viral DNA multiplies by riding along with the host's doubling.

29
00:02:06.603 --> 00:02:08.623
This path is the lysogenic cycle.

30
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.177
The prophage can stay for many generations.

31
00:02:13.497 --> 00:02:18.437
But something such as damage to the cell's DNA can switch it into the lytic cycle.

32
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.560
In that one cell, the host now builds new phages.

33
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.140
The cell bursts and dies.

34
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.940
The other cells carry on, prophage and all.

35
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.703
So the two paths do not have to end the same way.

36
00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:35.863
The lytic path builds new phages now.

37
00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:42.023
The lysogenic path copies only the phage DNA, maybe for many generations.

38
00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:47.423
New phages come only if it switches to lytic, which may never happen.

39
00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.107
Here is the rule to keep.

40
00:02:51.347 --> 00:02:53.007
A virus never builds itself.

41
00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:58.967
For lambda's two paths, a host cell builds new phages only on the lytic path.
